Merecat started out as a pun at Mongoose, but is now useful for actual web
serving purposes. It is however not a real Meerkat, merely yet another copycat,
forked from the great thttpd created by Jef Poskanzer.

Merecat httpd expands on the features originally offered by thttpd, but still
has a limited feature set:

  - Virtual hosts
  - Basic .htpassd and .htaccess support
  - URL-traffic-based throttling
  - CGI/1.1
  - HTTP/1.1 Keep-alive
  - Built-in gzip deflate using zlib
  - HTTPS support using OpenSSL/LibreSSL, works with Let's Encrypt!
  - Dual server support, both HTTP/HTTPS from one process
  - HTTP redirect, to gently redirect from HTTP server to HTTPS
  - Reverse proxy (proxy-pass), to front local app servers like nginx does
  - Native PHP support, using php-cgi if enabled in merecat.conf

The resulting footprint (~140 kiB) makes it quick and suitable for small and
embedded systems!
